Barnaby was not a cat of action. He was a cat of routine, a creature of sunbeams and saffron cushions, whose greatest daily peril was the slight tilt of his water bowl. He was a ginger tabby of substantial girth and even more substantial laziness, content to watch the world from the safety of his windowsill in the quiet suburb of Oakhaven.

But the universe, it seems, has a sense of humor.

It began on a Tuesday, with a butterfly. It was a Monarch, bright orange against the gray sky, fluttering erratically against the glass. Barnaby’s pupils dilated. His tail twitched. With a grunt of effort that sounded suspiciously like a sigh, he pushed the screen door open. It had been stuck for three years, but today, perhaps due to a gust of wind or a shift in the house’s foundation, it gave way.

Barnaby stepped onto the porch. The air smelled different here—sharper, laced with pine needles and damp earth. He took one step onto the grass, then another. The grass was cold and wet against his paws, a sensation he hadn’t felt since kittenhood. He shook his paw, offended, but the butterfly was already moving, drifting toward the old oak tree at the edge of the yard.

Barnaby followed. He told himself he was just stretching his legs. He told himself he would be back in five minutes.

He crossed the lawn, his belly brushing the dew. He reached the fence, a weathered wooden barrier that marked the boundary between his known world and the Wild Beyond. Usually, he would turn back. But the butterfly landed on the top rail, wings opening and closing like a slow heartbeat. Barnaby looked up. He looked down. He looked at the gap between the slats.

With a maneuver that surprised even him, he squeezed through.

The world beyond the fence was a labyrinth of overgrown brambles and towering ferns. It was loud. Birds chirped with aggressive urgency. Squirrels chattered insults from the canopy. Barnaby felt a strange thrill in his chest, a vibration that wasn’t quite fear, but something closer to electricity.

He followed the butterfly deeper into the woods. The path narrowed, becoming a deer trail. The light filtered through the canopy in dappled patches, creating a mosaic of gold and shadow. Barnaby moved with a grace he didn’t know he possessed, his paws silent on the moss. He felt light. He felt fast.

Then, he heard it. A low, guttural growl.

