Mittens was not a brave cat. She preferred sunbeams, warm laps, and the predictable rhythm of breakfast at seven, dinner at six, and naps in between.

But one rainy Tuesday, a blue butterfly landed on the windowsill outside her window.

Mittens blinked.

The butterfly fluttered once, twice, then flew straight through the slightly open window.

Mittens sat up.

She had never chased a butterfly indoors before. She had chased many things—moths, dust motes, the occasional sock—but never a butterfly that seemed to know exactly where it was going.

It landed on the back of the sofa.

Mittens crept after it.

It flew to the bookshelf.

Mittens followed.

It landed on the door handle.

Mittens stared.

Then, with a soft click, the door opened.

Mittens froze. The door had never opened by itself before. She looked at the butterfly, which hovered in the hallway as if waiting for her.

She took one cautious step forward.

Then another.

The house was quiet. Too quiet. The hallway stretched longer than she remembered, the walls lined with doors she had never noticed before. The butterfly glowed faintly, like a tiny blue lantern.

Mittens followed.

At the end of the hall was a door she had never seen before. It was made of dark wood and carved with strange symbols that looked like paw prints and stars.

The butterfly landed on the handle.

Mittens reached out a paw.

The door opened.

Beyond it was a forest.

Not the small patch of trees behind the house, but a vast, wild forest with silver leaves and rivers that sparkled like glass. The air smelled of moss and rain and something else—something old and wild.

Mittens stepped through.

The door closed behind her.

She turned, but the door was gone. There was only a tree with silver bark and a knot that looked almost like a face.

Mittens sat down.

She was not frightened, exactly. She was curious.

The butterfly circled her once, then flew deeper into the forest.

Mittens followed.

She crossed a bridge made of woven branches. She passed mushrooms that glowed in the dark. She heard birds singing in languages she almost understood.

At last, she came to a clearing where a great stone lion sat among the ruins of an ancient temple.

The lion opened one golden eye.

“Little cat,” it rumbled, “why have you come?”

Mittens sat down and wrapped her tail around her paws.

“I followed a butterfly,” she said.

The lion laughed, and the ground trembled gently.

“Many have come this way,” it said. “Few return.”

Mittens looked at the butterfly, which rested on a stone paw.

“Will you help me go home?” she asked.

The lion studied her for a long moment.

“Only if you bring me something from your world,” it said. “Something small, but precious.”

Mittens thought.

She thought of sunbeams, of warm laps, of the sound of her human humming while she cooked. She thought of the soft blanket by the radiator and the bowl that always smelled of tuna.

Then she reached into her memory and pulled out a single purr.

It was small and golden, like a warm coin.

She placed it before the lion.

The lion closed its eyes and listened.

“Good,” it said. “You may return.”

The forest shimmered.

Mittens blinked.

She was back in her living room. The window was open. Rain tapped against the glass. The butterfly was gone.

Mittens sat very still.

Then she looked down.

On the floor, where the butterfly had landed, lay a single silver leaf.

Mittens picked it up and carried it to her food bowl.

She did not know what it meant.

But she knew one thing.

Tomorrow, she would leave the window open again.
